      "problem": "$\\,$ $x$  is even and $y$ is prime.\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x$  is even and $y$ is prime\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\text{ is even and }y\\text{ is prime}) & ≡¬(x\\text{ is even}∧y\\text{ is prime}) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\text{ is even})∨¬(y\\text{ is prime}) \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is odd}∨y\\text{ is not prime} \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is odd or }y\\text{ is not prime}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
